## interpretation (from code, server.py system_status + supabase_store.get_tasks)
- system_status always returns {"status":"healthy"}; it calls storage.get_tasks(limit=500, scope=ALL_TENANTS).
- get_tasks swallows every exception and returns [] (supabase_store.py ~L900). So when the store is DOWN the endpoint
  either hangs until the client timeout (code 0 above) or answers 200 "healthy" with tasks_in_db=0 within ~4 ms once
  the storage path fails fast. The fast-200 phase coincides with storage breaker (5 failures / 10 s reset) behaviour;
  the exact mechanism of the fast failure was NOT instrumented — stated as consistent, not proven.
- => current healthCheckPath gives a FALSE-HEALTHY signal for an unavailable store, plus intermittent 5 s timeouts.
- /health/ready: 503 within <=1.0 s for the whole outage, 200 again 1 s after SIGCONT. Unambiguous.
- /health/live and /health: 200 throughout (process liveness only).

## Render consequence (rules applied to local samples; Render itself was NOT called)
- current path: stop routing once (37.1 s consecutive), no restart; then routing resumes while store still down.
- /health/ready: stop routing after 15 s AND restart after 60 s for an outage >= 60 s. On a single Free instance
  'stop routing' = full outage; restart on Free = ephemeral FS lost (PIN key in TMPDIR regenerated if
  AGOS_PIN_FINGERPRINT_KEY unset) and in-memory TASKS lost. Restart cannot fix an external store outage => restart loop risk.
- extra costs of the CURRENT path, from code: it is @rate_limit (100/60 s per client IP) and reads up to 500 task rows per probe.

## limits
- not Render: proxy, IP aggregation, checker cadence and free-plan health-check support are NOT measured.
- one outage shape (hang). A refused connection (process down) was not emulated.

